      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "During the first eight months of 1978, 26 patients were admitted to the Communicable Diseases Unit at King's Cross Hospital, Dundee with a diagnosis of campylobacter enteritis. The variety of clinical features encountered is described. Although diarrhoea occurred in all cases, it was preceded or accompanied by abdominal pain in the majority of cases and by fever in over half of the patients. The article emphasises the need to consider campylobacter infection in patients presenting with bloody diarrhoea, acute abdominal pain or pyrexias of unknown origin."},
